10.2.m:
In you is virile power, in you is luster;
be the lord of righteousness!
Be superior to your rivals;
yours is the milk-filled fortune!

To you let tributes come together;
to you let the tax be brought forth;
may Virāj yield milk for you;
let the great clans desire you!

May the lofty realm desire you;
brilliance is placed in your face.
Be dear to the gods;
in you are the cattle established.

In you is virile power, in you is luster;
in you is the sacrifice established.
Let the gods come to your call;
you are dear to Bṛhaspati.

Like fire, burn up the grass;
like a torrent, break the bank;
seize the fortune of your rivals,
like an edible root from a lotus!

10.3.m:
Like a horse, take the chariot;
like a lion, carry off the man;
like an elephant, break the reed-beds;
tear away the fortune of your rivals!

Bring here this khadira-wood,
and bring the tegana-wood;
and bring here this parṇa-wood;
bring here the strength of the draft-ox!

With them, be superior;
tear away the fortune of your rivals,
and carry off their milk!

10.4.m:
Let these kings slay the foes with arrows;
let these kings strike down the others in encounter;
let these kings conquer the armies;
I, the brahman-priest, strike down all scorners.

This realm is resourceful, heroic, victorious, formidable;
this realm is eager, of marvelous sound.
To this realm let others bring tribute;
I would serve the gods with oblation.

Like a rush of waters, crush the foes;
acting boldly from every direction, come together!
Of one vow, share out the wealth;
through the purohita, may the gods expand your realm!

10.5.m:
Thou art the overlord of amulets, a bull;
in thee has the lord of nourishment generated what is nourished;
in thee are all these prizes of victory, all riches.
O udumbara-tree, do thou overpower far from us
miserliness, folly, and hunger!

This amulet of udumbara-wood,
a hero, is bound on for a hero;
let it make our gain sweet-tasting,
and let it bestow upon us wealth with all heroic sons!

10.6.m:
May Bhaga place us today in good progress;
lead us hither onto the path of the gods!
Favorably turned, may his auspicious goodwill come to us;
then, through Bhaga, may there be kindling for us!

May Bhaga help me among cattle;
may Bhaga help me in grain!
Among dice, among women, may Bhaga [help] me;
may Bhaga help me with luster!

May Bhaga help me at evening;
may Bhaga help me by night!
May Bhaga [help] among those lying down;
in the morning may Bhaga come to me!

Bhaga from the gods,
Bhaga from mortals,
from sky, from earth,
from the intermediate space I choose Bhaga.
Let him, O Agni, rest in me;
may he assist me with luster!

10.7.m:
May the bull protect thee with cows;
may the stallion protect thee with swift steeds!
May Vāyu protect thee with the sacred word;
may Indra protect thee with virile powers!

May Soma protect thee with herbs;
may Sūrya protect [thee] with asterisms!
With months may Candramas, the slayer of Vṛtra, [protect] thee;
may Vāta guard [thee] with breath!

With breath they join Agni together;
Vāta is united with breath;
with breath, the all-facing
Sūrya did the gods generate.

Live with the life of the life-makers;
live, possessing full life, do not die!
Live with the breath of the ensouled;
do not go into the power of death!

10.8.m:
May the Ādityas protect me from the sky;
may the fires protect [me] from the earth;
may Indra and Agni protect me from the front;
may the two Aśvins grant safety all around;
may Jātavedas, the unslayable cow, protect across;
may the world-makers be my armor on all sides!

10.9.m:
May the waters cleanse me from ill-deed,
from the misfortunes that we have committed;
may we go forth pure with our bodies!

May the Gārhapatya fires purify me;
may the bold ones invoked by gods purify me;
may the Śakvarī verses backed by Soma purify me,
[and] the flowing ones of the plains!

May he purify me with the brāhmaṇa-pressings;
may he purify me with the royal-pressings;
a hundred strainers are stretched across;
with them may god Savitṛ purify me!

10.10.m:
Rising today, O thou of wondrous might,
strike down my rivals!
Strike them by day with thy rays;
kill them by night with darkness;
may they go to the lowest darkness!

10.12.m:
May Heaven and Earth torment him completely;
may he be cast down into the heated cauldron;
may he be bound in the snare of Nirṛti,
whosoever devises evil against us!

10.15.m:
Agni is the protector;
may he protect this army!
O supporter, stand firm;
may all my men be heroes!

Candramas is the protector;
may he protect this army!
O supporter, [stand firm] […]!

10.16.m:
Day is the protector;
may it [protect] this […]!
O supporter, [stand firm] […]!

Night is the protectress;
may she [protect] this […]!
O protectress, [stand firm] […]!

The Ocean is the protector;
may it […] this […]!
O supporter, [stand firm] […]!

Parameṣṭhin is the protector;
may he protect this army!
O supporter, stand firm;
may all my men be heroes!

The All-Gods are the protectors;
may they protect this army!
O supporters, stand firm;
may all my men be heroes!

11.1.m:
A bull, I place an embryo in the womb
for thee who longest for a bull,
if, besieged by the gods,
thou findest neither offspring nor progeny.

From Agni, from Tvaṣṭṛ, from Varuṇa,
from Indra, from Soma, from Bṛhaspati,
for thee who desirest a son,
I have fashioned a son, offspring from the gods.

11.2.m:
From the bow, from the bowstring, from the arrow,
from the brace, from the two arms,
from the barb, from the horn, from the neck,
I destroy thy decay!

From the point, from the feathering,
from the binding, and from the shaft,
from the notch, from the bowstring, from the arrow,
I destroy thy decay!

11.4.m:
From the sky protect me, from the earth protect me,
from the atmosphere protect me, from the plants protect me;
from the past protect me, and also from the future;
from every direction may jaṅgiḍa protect us!

11.5.m:
He has stretched out the thousandfold thread;
he has attained ten Agniṣṭomas,
who giveth the cow with afterbirth and calf;
him indeed the gods cause to ascend to heaven.

The cow with afterbirth, surrounded with hair,
the living man gave in battle at the beginning;
kindly to us, powerful, auspicious, enter here;
do not injure us with divine wrath!

Five gods drink forth this [cow]:
Indra, Tvaṣṭṛ, Varuṇa, Mitra, and Agni;
they all gave this [cow] to me;
living by her here, prolong thy life!

This Kaśyapa, Jamadagni, Vasiṣṭha—
the ṛṣis gave her to us in the beginning;
Bharadvāja, Gotama, Atri [gave] her, kindly to us;
may Viśvāmitra prolong the life of the giver!

11.6.m:
'Restorer' by name is your mother,
'Restorer' by name is your father;
ye are swift, winged;
whatever paineth, ye are its remedy.

11.8.m:
Time, a horse with seven reins, draws,
thousand-eyed, unaging, rich in seed;
him the wise poets mount;
his wheels are all the worlds.

This Time bears seven wheels;
seven are his navels; immortality is verily the axle;
he, anointing all these worlds,
Time, moves along as the first god indeed.

A full jar is set upon Time;
him indeed we behold existing manifoldly;
he, facing all these worlds—
him they call Time in the highest firmament.

He alone being, he brought the worlds together;
he alone being, he went around the worlds;
being the father, he became their son;
than him verily there is no other higher splendor.

Time created prosperity;
in Time the sun burns;
Time indeed is all beings;
in Time the eye sees around.

Time created the creatures,
Time in the beginning [created] Prajāpati;
the self-existent Kaśyapa [arose] from Time;
austerity was born from Time.

11.9.m:
From Time the waters arose;
from Time the Brahman, austerity, the quarters;
by Time the sun rises;
in Time it sets again.

By Time the wind purifies;
by Time the great earth,
the great heaven is placed in Time;
Time indeed, what has been and what is to be,
as a son, begot beforehand.

From Time the verses arose;
the sacrificial formula was born from Time;
Time set the sacrifice in motion,
an imperishable portion for the gods.

In Time are the Gandharvas and Apsarases;
in Time the worlds are placed;
in Time this divine Aṅgiras
and Atharvan preside.

11.10.m:
Whoever measures out the heap of Indra,
let him increase [the yield] of the threshing floor;
let him take increase on the threshing floor,
and rear many cattle.

Crushed was the vegetable on which he lay;
given was the wild beast;
the crippled lie writhing in pain,
who ate the unoffered oblation.

11.11.m:
Becoming a lion, it slays the cattle;
becoming fire, [it consumes] the grain;
the heap of Indra, unprepared,
also falls upon the deceiver.

11.12.m:
In heaven is thy tuft, O herb;
upon the earth art thou fixed;
with thee, thousand-jointed,
we prolong our life.

Overpower our adversary;
overpower those who fight [against us];
overpower all ill-wishers;
make many well-wishers for me!

11.13.m:
Thou goest beyond the earth with might;
thou sittest lovely upon the altar at the sacrifice;
thee the ṛṣis bore as a purifier;
do thou purify our misdeeds away from us!

11.14.m:
Let the earth, dripping with life, rise up;
let the wind blow upon her, cool and bearing seed;
let the earth, being in her season, receive
the seed, causing shoots of a thousand tendrils to mount up!

Propitiously pull the strap;
propitiously raise the goad;
propitiously let the plowshare glow;
propitiously let the plow draw!

Yoke the draft-beasts; stretch out the yokes;
in the prepared field sow here the seed;
so let Dhātṛ, so let Bhaga,
so let the two Aśvins make it,
so let the goddess Sarasvatī!

Let Parjanya stand swelling;
the sun rises together with his back-strider;
the barley causing delight,
repelling all hostilities.

Yawn open, [O furrow], not hurting the earth;
spread thy wings, become soft;
let the grain grow auspiciously!

11.15.m:
Drive away, drive off, driving the cows;
find refreshment here, make greatness!
Let not the plow-pin break for you;
let not your plow be shattered!

Let Deṣṭrī, the ocean, and Sinīvālī
speed our agriculture;
these five quarters that are—
let the wind speed them;
like the warriors at the ridge of the roof,
let the rains pour fiercely!

Propitiously let the plowman follow;
propitiously let the plowshare go cleaving the earth;
O Śunāsīra, thriving on the oblation,
do ye two make the grains propitious for us!

11.16.m:
Upward-resting indeed by name are these waters, which are herbs and forest trees;
Agni is their overlord.
He who knows these upward-resting waters and Agni as overlord—
just as these stand upright serving him,
even so do they stand upright serving him;
he becomes the overlord both of his own people and of others, who knows thus.

Trickling indeed by name are these waters, which are hoarfrost;
Āditya is their overlord.
He who knows these trickling waters and Āditya as overlord—
just as these trickle down when he rises,
even so do they trickle toward him as he approaches;
he becomes the overlord […].

Hastening indeed by name are these waters, which are streams;
the Earth is their sovereign lady.
He who knows these hastening waters and the Earth as their sovereign lady—
just as these go hastening when driven forward upon her,
even so his haters go driven away by him;
he becomes the overlord […].

Controlling indeed by name are these waters, which flow;
Varuṇa is their overlord.
He who knows these controlling waters and Varuṇa as overlord—
just as he takes control of these as they flow,
even so he takes control of his haters;
he becomes the overlord […].

Invigorating indeed by name are these waters, which are cows;
Tvaṣṭṛ is their overlord.
He who knows these invigorating waters and Tvaṣṭṛ as overlord—
he becomes possessed of vigor, possessed of brilliance;
he obtains cattle by the thousands;
he becomes the overlord […].

Lustrous indeed by name are these waters, which are ghee;
Pūṣan is their overlord.
He who knows these lustrous waters and Pūṣan as overlord—
he becomes possessed of luster, possessed of brilliance,
and his face shines even though not anointed;
he becomes the overlord […].

Mighty indeed by name are these waters, which are honey;
Indra is their overlord.
He who knows these mighty waters and Indra as overlord—
he becomes possessed of might, full of heroic power, possessed of virile power;
he obtains the honey-mixture in the royal assembly;
he becomes the overlord […].

Fierce indeed by name are these waters, which are hailstones;
the Maruts are their overlords.
He who knows these fierce waters and the Maruts as overlords—
he becomes fierce, strong;
people say of him, "He is a host of the Maruts";
he becomes the overlord […].

Great indeed by name are these waters, which are rain;
Parjanya is their overlord.
He who knows these great waters and Parjanya as overlord—
he becomes possessed of greatness, one who sustains his friends,
and his own people rejoice at him, saying, "He is ours";
therefore everyone is exalted when it rains;
he becomes the overlord […].

Surpassing in pride indeed by name are these waters, which are surā-liquor;
the two Aśvins are their overlords.
He who knows these waters surpassing in pride and the two Aśvins as overlords—
he surpasses his rivals; his rivals do not surpass him;
therefore one drunk surpasses another drunk;
he becomes the overlord […].

Poured into the trench indeed by name are these waters, which are in the furrow;
Yama is their overlord.
He who knows these waters poured into the trench and Yama as overlord—
both his own people and the clansmen submit to him round about;
he becomes the overlord […].

Delightful indeed by name are these waters, which are women;
Desire is their overlord.
He who knows these delightful waters and Desire as overlord—
people delight in him; he becomes lovely like the desire of women;
he becomes the overlord […].

All-supporting indeed by name are these waters, which are man;
Death is their overlord.
He who knows these all-supporting waters and Death as overlord—
he becomes a supporter of all; all supports him;
his three fires are placed in the house: the Southern Fire, the Gārhapatya, and the Āhavanīya;
four pleasant things come to him: an ornament, a bronze cup, a mule, and an elephant;
he becomes the overlord […].

12.1.m:
Go to the mountain, you are mountain-born;
on the mountain is your buffalo home.
Seek a wanton female slave;
shake her thoroughly, Takman!

12.3.m:
Let Viṣṇu prepare the womb;
let Tvaṣṭṛ shape the forms;
let Prajāpati pour in;
let Dhātṛ place the embryo in you.

Let King Varuṇa place an embryo in you,
let the god Bṛhaspati place an embryo,
let Indra and Agni place an embryo in you,
let Dhātṛ place an embryo in you.

We untie your knot;
let Dhātṛ place an embryo in you;
let a son ascend into the womb;
let him be born in accordance with the time of birth.

12.5.m:
From the climbing aśvattha-tree
the fire-drills were made;
from it, for you who are born,
Agni of strong jaws was born for Agni.

12.6.m:
Let your rivals wither with their roots dug up;
I call Agni out of their bodies.
With an oblation I block their breaths;
thus non-release is made for them.

12.8.m:
I know you, O apsarases, as wives;
you, O gandharvas, are the husbands!
Step away from the man;
immortal ones, do not cling to a mortal!

12.9.m:
They call this sterile cow Speech, [saying] “she is sterile”;
three sterile cows are set high in the common seat.
Of these I offer one with my mind in the fire;
let the Lord of Beings make her sweet for us!

Let Savitṛ make this sweet for us;
let the begetter of cattle make this sweet for us!
Offer, Agni, knowing the paths;
let the Lord of Beings make her sweet for us!

This is the third [oblation], you who have control, eat the sterile cow;
the embryo entered into you for greatness.
Willing, go to the willing gods;
let the desires of the sacrificer come true!

Supported by the water, pressed by the prize-offering, this is the cow
in whom Indra and Varuṇa shine;
she accompanies manly strength and weakness;
the wise beast entered into heroic power.

The sterile cow gave birth to the ancient, wise one;
the sterile cow gave birth to the yearling touching heaven;
the sterile cow gave birth to the youth at the distribution;
the sterile cow [gave birth to] the conqueror of riches.

12.10.m:
One became the form around [it];
one is called King by name;
one is the form of the husband’s form;
one is the form of the righteous.

12.11.m:
At every step the Ādityas and Aṅgirases
prepared the sacrificial formula;
they followed after the sterile cow;
that is called the Iḍā-sāman.

The sterile cow is Iḍā, the sterile cow Anumati,
the sterile cow they call Sarasvatī;
they regard the sterile cow as Virāj;
speech is the sterile cow, the earth the sterile cow.

The sterile cow is Deṣṭrī, Sinīvālī,
the sterile cow the dawn, Nirṛti the sterile cow;
death entered into the sterile cow;
the sterile cow entered into death.

I would not keep her [back];
I would give [her] away, and she would be mine;
for I know her teats,
her milk, the caul, and the afterbirth.

The skin is the womb, the upper garment the afterbirth,
the caul the egg, her headcloth the navel;
let the sovereign one suckle the mother unto old age;
fashioned by the sacred singers, he indeed is his kin.

12.12.m:
[He slew] the serpent clinging to the mountain;
Tvaṣṭṛ fashioned for him the resonant vajra;
like lowing milk-cows flowing forth,
the waters went straight down to the sea.

Acting like a bull, he chose the soma;
he drank of the pressed [juice] at the Trikadrukas;
the bounteous one grasped the missile vajra;
he slew him, the firstborn of serpents.

Like a non-warrior, in mad arrogance he challenged
the great hero, the repeller, the consumer of the residue;
he did not escape the collision of his blows;
crushed to pieces, Indra's enemy was shattered.

Vṛtra's mother was humbled;
Indra brought down his deadly weapon upon her;
above was the mother, below was the son;
Dānu lies like a cow with her calf.

In the midst of the courses of the [waters]
never standing still, never resting, the body was laid;
the waters wander over the hidden corpse of the wolf;
Indra's enemy lay down in long darkness.

12.13.m:
A horse's tail-hair you became, O Indra, then,
when the god alone [struck] you with the missile;
you won the cows, O hero, you won the soma;
you released the seven rivers to flow.

Indra is the king of what moves and what is at rest,
of the horned and the hornless, the vajra-armed;
he indeed rules as king of the peoples;
like a rim the spokes, he encompasses them.

12.14.m:
Of whom, the terrible one, they ask: 'Where is he?'
and also say of him: 'He does not exist';
he sweeps away the prosperities of the stranger like the stakes [of a game];
give [reverence] to him: he, […].

He who slew all those who bore great sin
constantly, unaware;
who yields not to the arrogant;
who is the slayer of the Dasyu: he, […].

12.15.m:
Even Heaven and Earth bow down to him;
even the mountains are terrified of his fury;
who is the soma-drinker, observed, vajra-armed,
who has the vajra in his hand: he, […].

12.16.m:
May Indra and Agni be for our welfare with their aids;
propitious to us be Indra and Varuṇa, to whom oblations are given;
propitious be Indra and Soma for winning welfare, for peace and blessing;
propitious to us be Indra and Pūṣan in the winning of the prize.

Propitious to us be Bhaga, and propitious to us be the praise;
propitious to us be Purandhi, and propitious be riches;
[propitious] to us be the praise of truth, well-guided;
propitious to us be Aryaman, born of many.

Propitious to us be the Creator, and propitious be our Upholder;
propitious to us be the wide [Earth] with her inherent powers;
propitious be the two lofty World-halves, propitious to us the pressing-stone;
propitious to us be the well-invoked [powers] of the gods.

Propitious to us be Agni whose face is light;
propitious Mitra and Varuṇa, propitious the two Aśvins;
propitious to us be the good deeds of the well-doers;
propitious to us may the vigorous wind blow.

Propitious to us be Heaven and Earth at the early invocation;
propitious for us to behold be the intermediate space;
propitious to us be the plants, the trees of the forest;
propitious to us be the victorious lord of the mist.

Propitious to us be the divine Indra with the Vasus;
propitious Varuṇa of good praise with the Ādityas;
propitious to us Rudra with the Rudras, healing with waters;
propitious to us may Tvaṣṭar with the divine women hear here.

Propitious to us be Soma, propitious to us the formulation;
propitious to us the pressing-stones, and propitious be the sacrifices;
propitious to us be the measurements of the sacrificial posts;
propitious to us the shoots, and propitious be the altar.

Propitious to us may the wide-eyed Sūrya arise;
propitious to us be the four cardinal directions;
propitious to us be the steadfast mountains;
propitious to us the streams, and propitious be the waters.

Propitious to us be Aditi with her ordinances;
propitious to us be the Maruts, singing well;
propitious to us be Viṣṇu, and propitious to us be Pūṣan;
propitious to us the world, and propitious be Vāyu.

Propitious to us be the divine Savitar who protects;
propitious to us be the Dawns shining forth;
propitious to us be Parjanya for our offspring;
propitious to us, bringing welfare, be the Lord of the Field.

12.17.m:
Propitious to us be the lords of truth;
propitious to us the coursers, and propitious be the cows;
[propitious] to us the Ṛbhus, skillful, fair-handed;
propitious to us be the Fathers in our invocations.

Propitious to us be the gods, the All-gods;
propitious be Sarasvatī along with poetic visions;
propitious those who attend, and propitious those who attend with gifts;
[propitious] to us the heavenly, the earthly, and propitious to us those of the waters.

Propitious to us be the divine Aja Ekapād;
propitious Ahi Budhnya, propitious the ocean;
propitious to us be the Son of Waters, the fruitful swell;
propitious to us be Pṛśni, guarded by the gods.

12.18.m:
Yoked, convey [the oblation] in front, O Jātavedas;
O Agni, take notice of this [work] as it is being performed.
You are the physician and the maker of the remedy;
through you may we gain cow, horse, and man.

12.20.m:
May the sorcerer find no pitier;
let the intermediate directions become spaceless for him!
Let both heaven and earth burn him together;
right here burn him down, O Jātavedas!

Always in flower, always in fruit,
always protected by Indra;
let the Piśācas ever die;
let not one of them be left over!

12.21.m:
Break open my rivals, O darbha-grass;
break open those who battle against me;
break open my evil-hearted [foes];
break open my haters, O amulet!

Cut down my rivals, O darbha-grass;
cut down those who battle against me;
cut down all my evil-hearted [foes];
cut down my haters, O amulet!

Hew down my rivals, O darbha-grass;
hew down those who battle against me;
hew down all my evil-hearted [foes];
hew down [my haters, O amulet]!

Cut off my rivals, O darbha-grass;
cut off those who battle against me;
cut off my evil-hearted [foes];
cut off my haters, O amulet!

Crush to pieces my rivals, O darbha-grass;
crush to pieces those who battle against me;
crush to pieces my evil-hearted [foes];
crush to pieces my haters, O amulet!

Pierce my rivals, O darbha-grass;
pierce those who battle against me;
pierce all my evil-hearted [foes];
pierce my haters, O amulet!

12.22.m:
Pierce through my rivals, O darbha-grass;
pierce through those who battle against me;
pierce through all my evil-hearted [foes];
pierce through my haters, O amulet!

Split open my rivals, O darbha-grass;
split open those who battle against me;
split open all my evil-hearted [foes];
split open my haters, O amulet!

Smash my rivals, O darbha-grass;
smash those who battle against me;
smash all my evil-hearted [foes];
smash my haters, O amulet!

Crush my rivals, O darbha-grass;
crush those who battle against me;
crush all my evil-hearted [foes];
crush my haters, O amulet!

Churn up my rivals, O darbha-grass;
churn up those who battle against me;
churn up all my evil-hearted [foes];
churn up my haters, O amulet!

Grind down my rivals, O darbha-grass;
grind down those who battle against me;
grind down all my evil-hearted [foes];
grind down my haters, O amulet!

Singe my rivals, O darbha-grass;
singe those who battle against me;
singe all my evil-hearted [foes];
singe my haters, O amulet!

Burn up my rivals, O darbha-grass;
burn up those who battle against me;
burn up all my evil-hearted [foes];
burn up my haters, O amulet!

Strike down my rivals, O darbha-grass;
strike down those who battle against me;
strike down all my evil-hearted [foes];
strike down my haters, O amulet!

They call you the gods' armor;
they call you, O darbha-grass, Brahmaṇaspati;
they call you Indra's armor;
you protect realms.

13.1.m:
Interposed for me is the broad intermediate space;
interposed are the mountains, my fires.
Let this sorcery not succeed for them;
I strike them back with an amulet-cord!

Fervor and toil are interposed around me;
I place the Vedic sacrifice interposed from me;
[…]

Do you two, Agni and Soma, be interposed from me;
I make heaven and earth my armor;
[…]

Interposed for me is the chanted Sāman;
interposed is Parameṣṭhin Prajāpati;
[…]

Interposed is the Serpent Queen, my heroes;
interposed is the sacrificial man for me;
[…]

Interposed for me are the six broad concordant [regions];
interposed are the Sādhyas, the Āptyas for me;
[…]

Interposed for me are the wise ones;
interposed are Sūrya [and] Mātariśvan;
[…]

Interposed for me are the flowing rivers;
interposed are the flowering plants for me;
[…]

Interposed are the cattle, my girth-animals;
interposed is the bird that flies;
[…]

Interposed for me are the arrows of the brāhmaṇas;
interposed are the trees with their roots;
[…]

13.2.m:
Interposed are the strongholds, the gods' resting places, before me;
interposed is the Jagatī among metres for me;
[…]

Interposed for me are the twelve oceans;
interposed are the stars at dawn for me;
[…]

Interposed are the dhiṣṇya fires for me;
interposed are the seasons and the seasonal periods for me;
[…]

Interposed for me are the four intermediate quarters;
I place what has been and what is to be interposed.
Let this sorcery not succeed for them;
I strike them back with an amulet-cord!

13.3.m:
In the triddhiṣ-grass is your seat;
in the tripuṣi-grass is your home, O evil one!
Without respite the wind strikes you;
without respite [the rain] rains upon you.

Here the paidva-steed was born;
here is his wallowing-ground;
here the neighing beast [comes];
the poison goes to destruction.

Let the winds blow in all directions;
let the rains rain in all directions;
do you piss out the poison all around,
like a pit of a hundred streams.

13.4.m:
As soon as born, he neighed;
he shook off the caul;
with the neighing of the vigorous horse,
the serpents were struck down in different directions.

13.5.m:
The one for whom you sacrifice achieves his aim;
unassailed he dwells, obtaining heroic strength;
he flourishes, distress reaches him not.
O Agni, [in your friendship may we not come to harm!]

13.7.m:
I ask you about the dappled [cow] and the red one;
I ask you about the calf together with the mother;
I ask you directly about Indra,
and about the master of the assembly of assemblies.

13.8.m:
Thus some wander forth, thus
some [wander] southward, westward, northward;
some wander forward.
Of all of them the meeting is together here;
he, having become one, moves about knowing.

14.2.m:
The seven ṛṣis consecrated Bharata;
upon him they placed the realm accompanied by sacrificial gifts;
for offspring the gods [placed] Manu rich in life-breath;
may those […]!

The thoughts, finding the sun, have given birth;
the recitations of good praise have arrived through the sacred formulation;
the [waters] of the royal consecration have brought forth milk;
the god Savitṛ has impelled all worlds.

The inspired poet has taken his stand, has arisen, has been born;
he has dispersed the foes, he has raised his two arms;
he has smitten Nirṛti far away;
and Sūrya has united [with him] through his rays.

14.4.m:
Unstrung is the bow of the tufted one;
pointless also is his arrow.
His arrows have perished;
auspicious is the state of his quiver.

14.5.m:
I seize the fit for sacrifice, the willing one,
for well-being, full of vigor,
free of disease, easily cared for.
May every world among the gods be mine;
faithful, I cook the cow of a hundred rice dishes.

With a verse I set the pot upon the fire;
upon the earth I place you, the earth.
Bearing the waters and the meat, do not wobble;
may the piśācas not slay you with their darts!

14.6.m:
Go to heaven, O cow of a hundred rice dishes!
Become the path for a thousand;
become ten thousand, a hundred thousand;
be imperishable!
You have ascended to the heavenly world.

They partake of the cow of a hundred rice dishes in a hundred ways;
he who gives [her] ascends a hundred ascents.
The Vāmadevya and Naudhasa [sāmans] are her two wings;
she has settled upon the third firmament, on the high peak.

Possessed of drink-yielding feet, first is the cow of a hundred rice dishes;
truth indeed is your middle, immortality your head;
O goddess, you fill both heaven and earth!

Cosmic order indeed is her hips and the thighs in her body;
might indeed her arms, and that for strength;
properly her belly, and then her sides;
the cow of a hundred rice dishes has reached all worlds.

14.7.m:
Here is Viśvāmitra, Jamadagni, Atri,
Bharadvāja, Gotama, this Vasiṣṭha;
let these, with Kaśyapa as seventh,
first eat this cooked cow of a hundred rice dishes!

14.8.m:
Strike off his feet so that he cannot advance;
strike off his hands so that he cannot govern.
The robber who approaches,
let him turn back, crushed;
let him turn back, let him turn well back;
let him turn back on a dry stump!

14.9.m:
Make the snake blind, O Night, of standing smoke,
headless;
strike out the two eyes of the wolf;
strike the thief onto the wooden block!

15.1.m:
The fierce conqueror of the quarters, bestowing vigor,
pure on the bright day, vigorous;
may Indra, the overlord, bring us across from here;
let him maintain this dominion in me on all sides.

The eastern of the quarters, accompanied by fame, glorious;
the All-Gods with the rainy season of days, possessing heaven;
may this dominion, hard to overcome, be vigor,
unassailed, powerful, possessing power.

15.2.m:
Turn toward [us], O Earth,
together with the sacrifice, with milk;
sent forth, let Agni descend, not drunk up.

Refreshing food and nourishment I have taken from here,
in the womb of the sacrifice, in the abode of the buffalo;
let it enter into our cattle, into our offspring;
I abandon weariness, lack of food, and disease.
